\section{The Interference Graph and Its Invariant}

In Chaitin and colleagues' \emph{Register Allocation via Coloring}, a
vertex represents a live range and an edge means the adjacent ranges
must receive distinct registers. Begin with one class of $K$
interchangeable registers, with instructions whose inputs are read
before their outputs are written. Backward liveness gives the set of
values live after each instruction. For each definition $d$, add edges
from $d$ to the other values live afterward. Treat simultaneous live-ins
as jointly live at a synthetic entry if they have no defining
instructions in the graph. At a pure copy $d\gets s$, omit the edge
that \emph{this definition} would add between $d$ and $s$: after the
copy they hold the same value. Keep an edge if some other instruction
establishes interference, and record the copy as a preference regardless.
For example, a value used for the last time by $d$'s defining instruction
can share its register with $d$ in this model; a live input to a later
instruction cannot.

Simplification removes a vertex of current degree less than $K$ and
pushes it onto a stack. When reinstated in reverse order, it has fewer
than $K$ already colored neighbors and must have a free color. If no
such vertex remains, failure is not yet proved. Pereira's \emph{A Survey
on Register Allocation} describes Briggs, Cooper, and Torczon's
optimistic coloring: remove a potential spill anyway and wait until
selection to find out if its neighbors exhaust the colors. With two
registers, a four-cycle has no initial vertex of degree less than two,
yet is colorable: remove one vertex optimistically, color the remaining
path, and observe that the two neighbors of the removed vertex get the
same color.

\section{Why Copy Coalescing Needs a Guard}

Coalescing merges the endpoints of a copy preference. The merged vertex
inherits the union of the two neighbor sets, and a successful coloring
then makes the copy redundant. Noninterference of the endpoints is
necessary but not sufficient to preserve colorability. With $K=2$,
consider the path $a-u-v-b$. It is bipartite, yet identifying $a$ with
$b$ turns it into a triangle. In fact the two endpoints of the original
path must get opposite colors, so the allocator must pay for that copy
if it wants to avoid spilling under these constraints.

For ordinary non-precolored vertices in the uniform model, Briggs's
conservative test allows a merge if fewer than $K$ distinct neighbors
of the merged vertex have current degree at least $K$. To see the
logic, temporarily simplify away the low-degree neighbors: the merged
vertex then has fewer than $K$ remaining neighbors and is itself
simplifiable. The high-degree subgraph that remains, after removing the
merged vertex, is a subgraph of the original graph. The test is
sufficient, not necessary. George and Appel's iterated register coalescing,
also reviewed by Pereira, alternates simplification with coalescing so
that a move failing a test early may pass later. When a move preference
blocks progress, freezing it drops the preference from consideration
for merging, not the copy from the emitted program. The final selection
can still favor the same color if neither endpoint conflicts with it.

\section{Spilling Changes the Input Graph}

Chaitin's \emph{Register Allocation \& Spilling via Graph Coloring}
incorporates spill choice into the coloring process. A candidate
score compares the estimated dynamic cost of stores and reloads
against interference relief; degree is one cheap proxy for relief.
For example, if spilling $x$ costs 400 weighted accesses and its
degree is eight, while $y$ costs six accesses at degree three, the
ratios are $50$ and $2$. The heuristic favors $y$; it does not claim
that removing $y$ makes the residual graph colorable. The weights
should reflect where access code will be inserted: putting a reload
on a cold edge is not equivalent to putting it in a loop.

A failed color leads to a rewrite, not assignment of a new register
called ``memory.'' In a register-only instruction set, store the
definition into a stack home and reload fresh virtual temporaries
around uses. Those reloads now have their own definitions and
interferences. Recompute liveness before trying again; reusing an old
graph can miss conflicts between newly introduced temporaries.
Keep trivially short mandatory reload ranges from being spilled back
into an endless sequence of reloads. If even these ranges cannot
occupy the registers required by a single instruction, investigate
the instruction form, register classes, or reserved scratch strategy
rather than counting on another coloring round. Chapter~\ref{ch:ext-234}
replaces the interchangeable-register assumption with target constraints.
